  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1059 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nDry weather continues throughout the Mid-South Monday evening\nwith temperatures steadily dropping amidst a very dry air mass.\nDewpoints currently sit between 35 - 45 F across the region which\nis expected to allow for rapid cooling overnight. Some sites have\nalready dropped to at or below to the new forecast NBM minimum\ntemperature, which is expected to continue across mainly West\nTennessee and northeastern Mississippi tonight. So, expect lows\nin the 40s with some areas, like West Tennessee and northeast\nMississippi, possibly dropping to near 40 for below. If areas are\nable to drop into the 30s patchy frost could occur, but\nconfidence is not high enough to mention tonight.\n\nTuesday will see more of the same dry and warm weather with\ntemperatures climbing back into the 70s and low 80s. An upper\nridge will begin to gradually amplify over the southern CONUS\nthrough the middle of the week, which alongside a persistent area\nof high pressure over the region, will lead to the continuation\nof dry and warm weather through the remainder of the week. So,\ndaily highs are forecast to climb into the 70s and 80s through\nthe forecast period. Dewpoints will also remain very low, leading\nto forecast afternoon relative humidities at or below 30% across\nthe majority of the region. Therefore, fire weather danger will\nremain elevated through the rest of the week.\n\nEnsembles are in very good agreement that the ridge will begin to\ntrudge east into this weekend, eventually getting flattened out\nby a trough over the northern Plains and Great Lakes region. The\nresulting pattern will then place the remnants of the ridge over\nthe Mid-South. Additionally, surface high pressure will remain in\nplace with southerlies retaining enough of an easterly component\nto recycle the current air mass back into the region. So, it\nseems that the warm and dry weather will persist through this\nweekend and into the end of the forecast period.",
